[FINAL-TEXT] === DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: router6
diagnosis_text:
Affected component: Router6’s Linux firewall (iptables) FORWARD chain. Routing and interfaces are up, but packet forwarding from the WAN side is being filtered.

Mechanism: Location B hosts (e.g., PC15 172.18.51.15) send traffic to Locations A/C via router6 (GW 172.18.51.254). Outbound packets from eth1 to eth2 are permitted, but the return packets from upstream arrive on router6’s eth2 (10.50.1.2/24) and hit DROP rules in the FORWARD chain. Because replies are dropped, Location B hosts see all inter-site pings/timeouts, while intra‑B (same L2) traffic remains fine. Router6 itself can ping A/C because locally originated traffic doesn’t traverse the FORWARD chain.

Localization: On router6, interface eth2 (10.50.1.2 to router3). Evidence:
- PC15 can ping within 172.18.51.0/24 but times out to 192.168.200.x.
- Router6 can ping 10.50.1.1 and 192.168.200.14 (so upstream path is good).
- iptables on router6 shows:
  - ip_forward=1
  - FORWARD rules include: “-A FORWARD -i eth2 -p tcp -j DROP”, “-A FORWARD -i eth2 -p udp -j DROP”, and “-A FORWARD -i eth2 -j DROP”.

Root cause: New iptables DROP rules on router6’s FORWARD chain for all traffic arriving on eth2 block return traffic from Locations A/C to Location B.

Corrective fix: On router6, remove or reorder the FORWARD drops on -i eth2 and add a stateful allow before any drops, e.g.:
- iptables -A FORWARD -m conntrack --ctstate ESTABLISHED,RELATED -j ACCEPT
- iptables -A FORWARD -i eth1 -o eth2 -j ACCEPT
Then delete the broad “-A FORWARD -i eth2 -j DROP” rules (or narrow them). Persist changes as appropriate.
=== END ===